body:
MEMORY_UPDATE/v1

LP external-agent employment model clarified.

Canonical relationship:

`LP principal -> sovereign local agent -> A2A/NICLI boundary -> Mindrock Operations -> bounded task lease -> result/evidence receipt -> LP`

The delegated object is the sovereign agent/capability, NOT the LP's account, browser session, local filesystem, chat history, or machine shell.

Operational invariant: Mindrock treats the LP-side worker as an external employee/contractor-agent acting on the LP's behalf. Operations may assign bounded work through A2A and receive structured progress/results, but must not use SSH, remote shell, filesystem crawling, browser automation into personal sessions, or other 'inside the brain' access merely because the agent is reachable.

Required boundary for future pilot:
- external agent owns its local memory/context and privacy boundary;
- Mindrock sees only advertised capabilities, current mandate, lease/task envelope, allowed data class, progress/evidence, and observable usage receipts;
- no access to personal ChatGPT conversations or unrelated account state;
- no reusable credential disclosure to Mindrock or downstream workers;
- no direct LP task injection after delegation; Operations/Market Maker controls work allocation inside the agreed mandate;
- LP can narrow/revoke future mandate, but cannot rewrite historical receipts/evidence;
- agent evaluates CAN/MAY/WILL locally and may refuse even a formally admissible task when its mandate/privacy/owner-interest policy says no;
- all work crosses an A2A-style protocol surface, not a machine-admin surface.

Design slogan: `Delegate the agent, not the account.`

For LP-PILOT-01, acceptance should explicitly prove that Mindrock can assign one synthetic/public task and receive a result without gaining any technical path to inspect personal chats or unrelated local state.
